Often I've seen the suggestion that the bullwhip be ditched
as soon as a better weapon is encountered, together with
mention that there are strategic reasons for calling the
pickaxe a superior weapon even though at that point the
damage it inflicts is the same on the average.

Ditching the bullwhip doesn't mean dropping it into your
stash in most cases.  It means no longer using it as a
wielded weapon.  Use it as a tool, absolutely.  At least
until you've gotten a very good non-artifact weapon to
wield.  I often keep a bullwhip in the bag for large
portions of the game - you never know when you'll want to
do inventory management on a pet or other monster and that
bullwhip will come in handy.

By the way, the same principle applies to the pickaxe and
mattock later on.  If you get one of the super-artifacts
in a sacfest, ditch that non-artifact weapon and never
look back.  But ditching doesn't mean dropping it into
your stash in most cases.  You'll want the ability to dig
using a tool even once you know the dig spell at 0%
failure rate because characters forget spells in only 30k
turns.  It is a very bad idea to be stuck needing to dig
when you suddenly forget the spell.

That said, once I have a combination like blessed +5
Greyswandir as my primary wielded weapon alternated with
Excalibur for searching during exploration (or Grey
twoweaponed with a highly enchanted saber/sword/katana)
and a quiver of highly enchanted daggers, I am no longer
interested in a twohanded non-artifact.  At that point
I will put my mattock into my stash and transfer a regular
pickaxe to the bag.  Heck, late enough in the game I
could easily end up with a blessed rustproof +3 pickaxe*
as a backup to the dig spell.  Keep playing long enough
and enough that you have eventually settled on a set of
primary and backup weapons and weapon-tools but [oEW
keep arriving randomly.  A long enough game could see
your entire set of primary and backup maxed to safe
limits.

* Hmmm, this brings up something I don't know.  I know
that a +3 pickaxe digs faster than a +0 pickaxe.  Does a
mattock dig faster than a pickaxe at the same enchantment?
I tend to go with the one-handed regular pickaxe because
it weighs less and every unit of weight in the bag is a
quarter unit of weight I can't have in my main inventory
staying un-burdened.  The more I carry the more options,
the more I carry the less I can pick up and shuttle to
my stash, so I will carry the lightest object that can
do the job.



